 ### Adaptive Segment Length
-**Definition**: A strategy to optimize speed while reducing artifacts by varying segment length based on contribution to ray color [S1, unofficial].
-**Steps**: Use smaller segments when the contribution to the ray color is large, and longer segments when the contribution is low [S1, unofficial].
+A strategy to optimize speed while reducing artifacts by varying segment length based on contribution to ray color [S1, unofficial]. Uses smaller segments when the contribution to the ray color is large, and longer segments when the contribution is low [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-Originating from the foundational work on direct volume rendering in the late 1980s and early 1990s, ray traversal methods evolved from simple fixed-step marching to adaptive and stochastic techniques [G]. The introduction of GPU fragment shaders enabled real-time image-order rendering loops [G]. Adaptive segment length strategies optimize speed while reducing artifacts by varying segment length based on contribution to ray color [S1, unofficial].
